<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lament · c. 1656 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn about Jesus suffering on the cross, sung in German and in English. Bach used its tune again and again.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Paul Gerhardt (German), from a medieval Latin poem now attributed to Arnulf of Leuven; English by James Waddel Alexander</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hans Leo Hassler, adapted by Johann Crüger</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German Lutherans; later Protestant churches across the English-speaking world</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latin, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Latin poem 12th or 13th century; German hymn first printed 1656; English translation 1830</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (Latin poem attributed to Arnulf of Leuven (d. 1250); German by Paul Gerhardt (1656); English by James Waddel Alexander (1830); tune by Hans Leo Hassler (1601))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sacred-head-now-wounded">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</a>&quot; is a Christian Passion hymn based on a long medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem, &quot;Salve mundi salutare&quot;, whose sections address in turn the parts of Christ's body hanging on the Cross. Its last part, from which the hymn is taken, addresses Christ's head and begins &quot;Salve caput cruentatum&quot;. The poem was long attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> (1091–1153) and is now attributed to the medieval poet Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> (died 1250). Dieterich Buxtehude chose stanzas from its seven cantos for the text of his Membra Jesu Nostri.</p><p>The Lutheran hymnist Paul Gerhardt (1607–1676) translated the whole poem into German, reworking it as a more personal contemplation of Christ's death on the cross. It first appeared in Johann Crüger's hymnal Praxis pietatis melica in 1656, and its closing section, beginning &quot;O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden&quot;, has become a hymn in its own right.</p><p>Its music had a secular start. Hans Leo Hassler wrote the tune around 1600 for a love song, &quot;Mein G'müt ist mir verwirret&quot;, printed in his Lustgarten Neuer Teutscher Gesäng in 1601; in 1656 Crüger took it for Gerhardt's hymn and simplified its rhythm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> arranged the melody and used five stanzas of the hymn in four settings in his St Matthew Passion. He also used it in his cantatas Sehet, wir gehn hinauf gen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, BWV 159, and Komm, du süße Todesstunde, BWV 161, and set it to other words in Parts I and VI of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Oratorio. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> arranged it for the sixth station of his Via crucis, Rued Langgaard wrote variations on it for string quartet, Edmund Rubbra used it in the final chorus of his Ninth Symphony, &quot;Sinfonia Sacra&quot;, and Mauricio Kagel quoted it at the end of his Sankt-Bach-Passion, written for the tercentenary of Bach's birth in 1985.</p><p>The first English translation, &quot;O Head so full of bruises&quot;, was made in 1752 by John Gambold, an Anglican vicar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>. The American Presbyterian minister James Waddel Alexander (1804–1859) translated it again in 1830 as &quot;O sacred head, now wounded&quot;, and his version became one of the most widely used in 19th- and 20th-century hymnals. Henry Williams Baker made another from the German for Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861, Catherine Winkworth one for her collection Lyra Germanica in 1855, and in 1899 the poet Robert Bridges translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> afresh; his version is used in the Episcopal hymnals of 1940 and 1982 and the New English Hymnal (1986). Karen Lynn Davidson's version, which draws on parts of 2 Nephi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, is sung in the hymnal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a>.</p><p>The tune has carried other words too, from Timothy Dudley-Smith's hymn &quot;We come as guests invited&quot; to secular songs: Tom Glazer's protest song &quot;Because All Men Are Brothers&quot; (1947), Paul Simon's &quot;American Tune&quot; and John K. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a>'s &quot;Stop Error&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A meditation on the suffering of Christ on the cross. The singer addresses the wounded, thorn-crowned head of Christ and dwells on his death as a personal grief. The mood is grave and sorrowful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O sacred Head, now wounded,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">voll Schmerz und voller Hohn,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with grief and shame weighed down,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">o Haupt, zum Spott gebunden</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now scornfully surrounded</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mit einer Dornenkron;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with thorns, Thine only crown;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">o Haupt, sonst schön gezieret</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O sacred Head, what glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mit höchster Ehr und Zier,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what bliss till now was Thine!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">jetzt aber höchst schimpfieret:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yet, though despised and gory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">gegrüßet sei'st du mir!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I joy to call Thee mine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Erscheine mir zum Schilde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Be Thou my consolation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">zum Trost in meinem Tod,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my shield when I must die;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">und laß mich sehn dein Bilde</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>remind me of Thy passion</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">in deiner Kreuzesnot.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when my last hour draws nigh.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Da will ich nach dir blicken,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mine eyes shall then behold Thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">da will ich glaubensvoll</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>upon Thy cross shall dwell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">dich fest an mein Herz drücken.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my heart by faith enfolds Thee.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wer so stirbt, der stirbt wohl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who dieth thus dies well.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Paul Gerhardt (1656); the first and last of his stanzas, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Sacred_Head%2C_Now_Wounded" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: tr. James Waddel Alexander (1830); the same two stanzas, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Sacred_Head%2C_Now_Wounded" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="vGHwyAG_sS8" data-title="O Sacred Head, Now Wounded: Vocalconsort München, the chorale from Bach's St Matthew Passion, in German"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Vocalconsort München, the chorale from Bach's St Matthew Passion, in German</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Vocalconsort München. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGHwyAG_sS8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Membra Jesu Nostri (Dieterich Buxtehude)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">text drawn from stanzas of the Latin poem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden (Paul Gerhardt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1656</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German translation, the hymn itself</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O Head so full of bruises (John Gambold)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1752</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O sacred head, now wounded (James Waddel Alexander)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1830</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ah wounded Head! Must Thou (Catherine Winkworth)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1855</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O sacred head surrounded (Henry Williams Baker)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O sacred Head, sore wounded (Robert Bridges)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1899</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation from the Latin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O savior, thou who wearest a crown (Karen Lynn Davidson)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">St Matthew Passion (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">five stanzas in four settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sehet, wir gehn hinauf gen Jerusalem, BWV 159 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">text and melody used</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Komm, du süße Todesstunde, BWV 161 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody as counterpoint and closing chorale</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Oratorio, Parts I and VI (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody with other words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Via crucis (Franz Liszt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement in the sixth station</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations for string quartet (Rued Langgaard)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sinfonia Sacra, Symphony No. 9 (Edmund Rubbra)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune in the final chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sankt-Bach-Passion (Mauricio Kagel)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1985</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted at the end</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We come as guests invited (Timothy Dudley-Smith)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new hymn to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Because All Men Are Brothers (Tom Glazer)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1947</span><span class="sg-chain-h">protest song to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">American Tune (Paul Simon)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stop Error (John K. Samson)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song to the tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Salve mundi salutare (attributed to Arnulf of Leuven)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">Middle Ages</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Latin poem the hymn translates</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mein G'müt ist mir verwirret (Hans Leo Hassler)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1601</span><span class="sg-chain-h">secular love song whose tune it took</span></li></ul></section>
